**One note before the program.** The Rhylthyme tools (`search_public_recipes`, `plan_event`, `validate_program`, `analyze_schedule`, `visualize_schedule`) are not reachable in this session, so I can't complete step 1 of the tool worklist (catalog lookup) and I can't quote a real Gantt or hand you a live URL — I won't invent either. What I *can* fix is the error `validate_program` reported: the `tracks` array was missing. Here is the complete program, with the hand-checked arithmetic spelled out so nothing rests on a tool call I couldn't make.

## The improved question

> "Which activities depend on which, and which can run at the same time?"

Better: **"For each activity, what must already be true — on the pass, on the floor, in the room, or on the clock — before it can start *well*, and what must be true at the instant it ends for the next thing to be any good? Which activities are held, staged or rested so something else is ready at the same moment, which single events release a burst of downstream work, and which one resource serialises work that would otherwise look parallel?"**

**Answered against this sheet:**

- **The pass serialises three courses.** Nothing on the pass overlaps, and the floor *waits* for it — so every serve is triggered off the end of its own plate-up, not off a clock. `plate-mains` sits five minutes *after* `clear-starters` (not after `serve-starters`), so the mains are cooked for plates that are actually back.
- **Two bursts release downstream work:** the canapés ending (`call-to-dinner` → `seat-guests` → the 15-minute pre-plate window → toast) and the speeches ending (`cut-cake` → `plate-dessert` → `serve-dessert` → dancing). Both are gates, not clock times.
- **Two holds are invisible unless asked about.** (a) The 15 minutes between the room being called and starters hitting the pass is not idle — it's the kitchen staging and holding the first course; that's a step, inferred. (b) Dessert is *held*, not plated early: I hung `plate-dessert` off the **start** of `cut-cake`, so if the speeches run to 25 minutes the cake cut slides, the dessert slides with it, and the pass is never holding a lamped plate. That's the sheet's own note, expressed as a trigger instead of a promise.
- **The microphone is the second serialiser.** Toast and speeches must never overlap; hence the inferred `mic-check` before the toast.
- **The clock is only the first twenty minutes.** The bar can open at the ceremony break and the canapés start "twenty minutes in" — everything else is chained to an event.

## Tracks (six parallel lines)

| track | line of work | steps, in order |
|---|---|---|
| `room` | the planner's room call | flip → dress → call-to-dinner → seat |
| `bar` | foyer | open-bar |
| `floor` | the two servers | canapés → serve/clear starters → serve/clear mains → serve dessert |
| `pass` | one kitchen pass | stage courses (inferred) → starters → mains → dessert |
| `mic` | the MC | mic-check → toast → speeches |
| `danceFloor` | the one dance floor | first dance → cake → dancing |

**Hand-computed itinerary** (defaults: canapés 40 min, speeches 15 min; clock from the 16:00 ceremony break): flip 16:00–16:45 · bar 16:00–16:20 · canapés 16:20–17:00 · room dressed 17:00 (exactly when the canapés land) · call 17:00–17:05 · seated 17:05–17:20 · toast 17:20–17:25 · starters plated 17:20–17:35, served 17:35–17:45, cleared 17:55–18:05 · mains plated 18:10–18:40, served 18:40–18:55, cleared 19:20–19:30 · first dance 19:30–19:35 · speeches 19:35–19:50 · cake 19:50–20:00 · dessert plated 19:50–20:05, served 20:05–20:15 · dancing from 20:25 to the band's last set. At the 25-minute speech ceiling everything from the cake onward slides 10 minutes and dancing starts 20:35 — the pass is untouched, which is the point of the hold. **Binding constraints: `pass` (one course at a time) and `mic` (one voice at a time); the canapé window is the only slack in the sheet, so I padded nothing else.**

